-- title:
--   Rational part of the Eisenstein quotient is torsion
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a natural number assumed prime (through a `Fact` instance), and let `hcomm : HeckeOperatorsCommuteBar p` be the hypothesis that the divisorial Hecke endomorphisms `heckeOperatorBar p ℓ` of `JZero p`, one for each rational prime $\ell$, commute pairwise. Here `JZero p` is the degree-zero divisor class group $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the geometric modular function field `modularFunctionFieldBar p`, the base change to $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ of the field generated over $\mathbb Q$ by the $q$-expansions $j(q^d)$ for $d \mid p$. Under `hcomm` the commuting operators make `JZero p` a module over `HeckeAlg` $= \mathbb Z[T_\ell : \ell \text{ prime}]$ via the instance `heckeModuleBar p`, which sends `heckeGen ℓ` to `heckeOperatorBar p ℓ`. The Eisenstein ideal `eisensteinIdeal p` is the kernel of the evaluation $T_\ell \mapsto 1+\ell$ ($\ell \nmid p$), $T_p \mapsto 1$; the Eisenstein kernel `eisensteinKernel (JZero p) (eisensteinIdeal p)` is the ideal of those $t \in$ `HeckeAlg` for which there exists $i$ in the Eisenstein ideal with $(1+i)t$ annihilating every element of `JZero p`, and `eisensteinKernelSubmodule p` is the submodule of `JZero p` generated by the images of this ideal. The set `eisensteinQuotientRational p (heckeModuleBar p)` is the image, in the quotient of `JZero p` by that submodule, of the set of classes $x$ such that $\sigma \cdot x - x$ lies in the submodule for every $\sigma \in \mathrm{Aut}_{\mathbb Q}(\overline{\mathbb Q})$. The theorem asserts that every element $z$ of this set is of finite order: there exists a natural number $n > 0$ with $n \cdot z = 0$. Thus the conclusion is elementwise torsion, not finiteness of the set.
--
--   Classically this is Mazur's theorem that the Eisenstein quotient $\tilde J$ of $J_0(p)$ has Mordell–Weil rank zero (Mazur, Modular curves and the Eisenstein ideal, III, Thm (3.1) and Cor (3.5)). The formal version differs from the textbook statement in two ways: the group acted on is the divisor class group of the geometric modular function field with the divisorial Hecke action supplied by `heckeModuleBar p` (which is the genuine action precisely under `hcomm`), the relevant ideal is the Eisenstein kernel rather than the Eisenstein ideal itself, and 'rationality' is taken in the weak form that $\sigma \cdot x - x$ lies in the Eisenstein-kernel submodule for all $\sigma$. It is the torsion half of the finiteness statement `EisensteinQuotientInvariantsFiniteAt p (heckeModuleBar p)`, i.e. of the finiteness of the set `eisensteinQuotientRational p (heckeModuleBar p)`, the other half being finite generation of the subgroup it generates.
